<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/3rd-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">First Punic War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/264-bce">264</a>–<a href="/years/241-bce">241 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The First Punic War was fought in 264–241 BCE between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, mainly over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>. Rome built a navy and won a decisive naval victory at the Aegates Islands, forcing Carthage to give up Sicily and pay an indemnity. It was the first major blow to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punic-empire">Punic Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/first-punic-war.jpg" alt="First Punic War" width="320" height="189" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Harrias · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:First_Punic_War_264_BC_v3.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> had been expanding in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> for a century and by 272 BCE had conquered the peninsula south of the River Arno. Carthage, with its capital in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>, dominated southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, much of the North African coast, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balearic-islands">Balearic Islands</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corsica">Corsica</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> and the western half of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>. Most of what is known of the war comes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a>, a Greek sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> as a hostage in 167 BCE, whose Histories were written about a century after the war.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The war began in 264 BCE when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> gained a foothold at Messana in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>. Syracuse allied with them, and a large Carthaginian army sent to lift the siege of Akragas was heavily defeated in 262 BCE. Land fighting was mostly sieges and raids, so the Romans turned to the sea. In 260 BCE they built a fleet using a shipwrecked Carthaginian quinquereme as a model, and they introduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corvus">corvus</a>, a bridge with a spike that let <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">legionaries</a> board enemy ships instead of ramming them.</p><p>In 256 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> sailed with 330 warships and won the Battle of Cape Ecnomus, possibly the largest naval battle in history by the number of combatants. Their invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> at first went well, but a storm later destroyed most of the fleet sent home, with over 100,000 men lost. They rebuilt repeatedly and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Panormus</a>. By 248 BCE Carthage held only Lilybaeum and Drepana, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamilcar-barca">Hamilcar Barca</a> held out from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eryx">Eryx</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> tactics. In 243 BCE the Romans built a new fleet and blockaded the garrisons, and in 241 BCE they destroyed the Carthaginian relief fleet at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-aegates">Battle of the Aegates Islands</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> paid large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-reparations">reparations</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> became a Roman province. Both states were exhausted. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> became the leading military power in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, and building about 1,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a> laid the base for its maritime dominance for 600 years. Carthage's unpaid foreign troops rebelled, and the revolt was put down with great difficulty and savagery. In 237 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> took Carthage's planned expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> as an act of war and made it cede Sardinia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corsica">Corsica</a> and pay a further 1,200 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a>. This resentment is considered a cause of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-punic-war">Second Punic War</a> of 218 BCE.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “First Punic War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The loss of sea control in the <b>First Punic War</b> proved irreversible.</blockquote><a href="/empire/punic-empire">Punic Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the <b>First Punic War</b> Rome, with little naval tradition, built fleets of a hundred or more warships at a time, and fitted them with a boarding bridge, the corvus, to win at Mylae in 260 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-republic">Roman Republic · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>First Punic War</b> Carthage expanded into the peninsula.</blockquote><a href="/culture/iberians">Iberians · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Carthage sued for peace, ending the <b>First Punic War</b> and ceding Sicily, Rome's first overseas province.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-10">10 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After further wars Falerii revolted in 241 BCE, just after the <b>First Punic War</b>, and was crushed within days.</blockquote><a href="/state/falerii">Falerii</a></li><li><blockquote>In 258 BCE, during the <b>First Punic War</b>, the Romans captured it and sold most inhabitants into slavery.</blockquote><a href="/state/kamarina">Kamarina</a></li></ul></section>
